      "gl_buckling": "Buckling",
      "gl_buckling_d": "When a long thin piece under squeeze suddenly snaps sideways, far below its crushing strength. Press a spaghetti noodle from both ends to feel it.",
      "gl_compression": "Compression",
      "gl_compression_d": "A squeezing force, like pressing on a soda can. Shown in blue.",
      "gl_governing": "Governing",
      "gl_governing_d": "Engineer-speak for \"the one that decides\". The governing member is the weakest link; the governing check is whichever fails first.",
      "gl_hint2": "Drag or arrow keys — orbit · Scroll or +/− — zoom",
      "gl_kn": "kN (kilonewton)",
      "gl_kn_d": "A unit of force. 1 kN is roughly the weight of a washing machine; a car weighs about 15 kN.",
      "gl_member": "Member / chord / diagonal",
      "gl_member_d": "Any single piece is a member. The long top and bottom pieces are chords; the zig-zags are diagonals.",
      "gl_mpa": "MPa (megapascal)",
      "gl_mpa_d": "Pressure: force concentrated on an area. The same push hurts more through a thumbtack than a book cover; that is higher MPa.",
      "gl_sf": "Safety factor",
      "gl_sf_d": "How many times stronger than needed. 1.0 = barely holds; 2.0 = holds twice the load. Real bridge codes demand 2\\u20136.",
      "gl_slender": "Slenderness",
      "gl_slender_d": "Length compared to thickness. A pencil is stubby; an uncooked spaghetti noodle is slender. Slender pieces buckle easily.",
      "gl_span": "Span",
      "gl_span_d": "The open distance the bridge must cross without support underneath.",
      "gl_tension": "Tension",
      "gl_tension_d": "A pulling force, like a tug-of-war rope. Shown in red.",
      "gl_truss": "Truss",
      "gl_truss_d": "A frame of triangles. Triangles cannot change shape without changing a side length, which makes them stiff.",
      "gl_yield": "Yield",
      "gl_yield_d": "The point where a material stops springing back and permanently bends or tears. Passing it is a strength failure.",
      "glossary_title": "The words on this page, in plain language",
      "hint_angle": "About 45\\u201360\\u00B0 is the sweet spot: steep enough to be short, shallow enough to share the load.",
      "hint_bays": "How many triangles the truss is divided into. More bays = shorter, sturdier pieces, but more joints to build.",
      "hint_bracing": "Cross-ties that stop the top chord flexing sideways. Fewer braces = longer free lengths = easier to buckle. This slider decides most verdicts here.",
      "hint_chord": "The biggest pull or squeeze in the long top/bottom pieces. (1 kN is about the weight of a washing machine.)",
      "hint_cost": "The engineering game: the CHEAPEST design that is still SAFE wins.",
      "hint_diag": "The biggest force in the zig-zag pieces. Diagonals work hardest near the supports.",
      "hint_height": "A taller truss spreads the load better, like a wider stance. Watch the safety factor rise as you raise it.",
      "hint_length": "All the pieces laid end to end. More length = more material = more cost.",
      "hint_load": "The weight pressing down at each top joint. Imagine trucks parked all along the deck.",
      "hint_mass": "What the bare structure weighs. A bridge must carry itself before it carries anything else.",
      "hint_pcr": "The push at which the weakest strut snaps sideways, like a spaghetti noodle pressed from both ends.",
      "hint_reaction": "How hard each end pushes back up. The two ends together carry the whole load.",
      "hint_slender": "Length divided by thickness. Long and thin = wobbly. Real design codes stop at about 200.",
      "hint_span": "The distance between the two supports. Longer spans are MUCH harder: forces grow fast with span.",
      "hint_stress": "Force squeezed into each bit of material. Must stay well BELOW the yield number beside it.",
      "hint_vehiclepos": "Slide the truck across the bridge and watch the forces chase it.",
      "hint_weakest": "The piece closest to buckling. Find it in the picture above; the whole bridge is only as safe as this one piece.",
      "hint_yield": "The stress where this material starts to permanently bend or tear. Your ceiling.",
      "intro_colors": "In every picture: red pieces are being STRETCHED (tension), blue pieces are being SQUEEZED (compression), and thicker means working harder.",
      "intro_dismiss": "Got it — hide this",
      "intro_step1": "Read the verdict card below the picture: is this bridge SAFE, MARGINAL, or FAILED, and why?",
      "intro_step2": "Drag any slider and watch the verdict, the colors, and the numbers respond instantly. Breaking it on purpose is a great way to learn.",
      "intro_step3": "Your goal: a \\u2713 SAFE bridge for the LOWEST material cost. That trade-off is the whole job of a structural engineer.",
      "intro_title": "Start here",
      "label_bays": "Bays (triangle sections)",
      "label_pcr": "Buckling load (P_cr)",
      "label_reaction": "Push-back at each support",
      "label_weakest": "Weakest strut",
      "verdict_failed_buckle_plain": "A long, thin piece would snap SIDEWAYS before the material itself ever failed. That is buckling, and it happens suddenly, with no warning creak.",
      "verdict_failed_yield_plain": "The material itself would be crushed or torn apart. That is a yield failure: too much force through too little material.",
      "verdict_marginal_plain": "It holds, but with less spare strength than real bridge codes demand (a safety factor of at least 2). An engineer would strengthen it before letting traffic on.",
      "verdict_safe_plain": "Two different checks, both passed: the material is strong enough (yield), and no piece is so long and thin that it would snap sideways (buckling).",
      "0_mph": "0 mph",
      "0_people": "0 people",
      "1_5_very_thin_margin": "1.5 (very thin margin)",
      "1_anchorages_first": "1. Anchorages first",
      "1_beam_elements_1d_line_elements_with_": "(1) BEAM elements: 1D line elements with bending + axial + sometimes torsion. Use for truss members, beams, frames. Fast. Best for slender members where length >> cross-section. (2) SHELL elements: 2D surface elements with bending. Use for plates, decks, walls, the body of a box girder. Captures both in-plane and out-of-plane behavior. (3) SOLID elements: full 3D bricks or tetrahedra. Use for chunky parts: bearings, pylon bases, weld details, foundations. Most accurate, most expensive. (4) CONTACT elements: model surfaces sliding or separating. Used for bearings, bolted joints, expansion joints. (5) SPRING / LINK elements: model stiffness without geometry — e.g., soil under a footing, cable in a stay.",
      "2_0_code_minimum": "2.0 (code minimum)",
      "2_towers_saddles": "2. Towers + saddles",
      "3_catwalks_pilot_ropes": "3. Catwalks + pilot ropes",
