      "camouflage": "🎨 Camouflage",
      "camouflage_and_mimicry_are_both_anti_d": "Camouflage and mimicry are both anti-detection strategies, but they work in different ways and serve different ecological functions.\n\nCAMOUFLAGE is concealing — making yourself hard to see. Cephalopod camouflage involves matching SUBSTRATE COLOR, PATTERN, and TEXTURE with the surrounding environment. The goal is to blend in: any predator scanning the area sees only the rock or sand or coral, not the cephalopod. Most octopuses, cuttlefish, and bottom-dwelling species rely heavily on camouflage.\n\nMIMICRY is impersonating — making yourself look like ANOTHER THING. The mimic octopus (Thaumoctopus mimicus) is the textbook example. When threatened, it doesn't hide. Instead it impersonates a different species: lionfish (spread arms in venomous-spine-like position), sea snake (extend two arms in a swimming serpentine motion), flatfish (flatten body + swim like a flounder). The chosen mimic depends on which predator is present — if a damselfish is around, it mimics lionfish (a predator the damselfish avoids).\n\nWhy both? Different ecological situations favor different strategies. Camouflage is most effective when (a) a good substrate match is available, (b) the cephalopod can stay still, and (c) predators hunt by sight. Mimicry is most effective when (a) substrate match is poor (the mimic octopus often lives on muddy sand-flat with little natural cover), (b) the cephalopod is exposed, and (c) predators avoid specific dangerous species.\n\nCamouflage is the more general strategy and used by more species. Mimicry requires specific cognitive flexibility — the mimic octopus has to recognize the threat AND choose which mimic is appropriate. This is cognitively expensive and likely emerged through long evolutionary history of being a sand-flat octopus that needed an alternative defense.\n\nA related strategy is APOSEMATISM — warning coloration. Blue-ringed octopuses flash vivid blue rings when threatened. This isn't mimicking anything; it's ADVERTISING actual danger (the octopus carries tetrodotoxin). Predators that have encountered + survived a blue-ringed octopus learn to avoid the ring pattern. Aposematism works only if the animal has a real defense that predators can learn to associate with the warning signal.\n\n(Sources: Hanlon, 2007 — Curr. Biol.; Norman et al., 2001 — Proc. R. Soc. B; Mäthger et al., 2008 — Curr. Biol.)",
      "camouflage_cryptic": "Camouflage (cryptic)",
